UDALS compensation for marble and mayfly

The implementation runs as the device-selected sensor-notifier extension. It
replaces the factory-lux RawLightNotifier; it must not run alongside it. CWB,
sensor activation and reporting belong to UdalsNotifier/Capture. Compensation
has no Android dependencies and is compiled separately for host validation.
Timeline pairs sensor events with captures and prior display state, using
CLOCK_BOOTTIME (the Android sensor timestamp clock).

Model provenance

The JSON calibration tables alone do not describe the stock screen emission
model. Both stock implementations also contain trained neural networks and
spatial weights. extract_model.py extracts only these parameters from the
stock citsensor implementation, through each device's extract-files.py. It
installs vendor/etc/sensors/udals-model.json as proprietary vendor data. The
stock service and its executable code are not installed or loaded.

Supported dumps:
  marble: OS3.0.4.0.VMRMIXM
  mayfly: OS3.0.3.0.VLTCNXM

The extractor verifies the complete ELF SHA-256 before reading parameters.
Do not update the accepted hash without revalidating the layout and math.

The active stock paths are:
  marble: gamma 2.2, three 19x19 grids of weights over 5x5 pixel blocks;
          four independent 4/128/64/32/1 networks, normalized inputs;
          calibrated C/R/G/B subtraction and TCS3701 lux conversion.
  mayfly: gamma 2.2, one 25x25 grid over 5x5 pixel blocks;
          logarithmic inputs, four-output networks selected at DBV 400/900
          and by DC mode below/equal to 400; TMD3719 lux conversion.

Both active paths scale predicted channel contributions by the interpolated
per-unit/reference WHITE table ratio. The RGB tables in marble's JSON are
not the source of the neural-network predictions. Missing per-unit calibration
leaves the stock model unscaled. Invalid/inaccessible calibration is reported
instead of being silently treated as missing. Channel scales are applied
after subtraction. Per-unit calibration files are never modified.

Stock-specific low-lux corrections, median windows and reporting thresholds
are retained. DBV is used directly in the normal range and capped at 2047 in
HBM; the backlight class's max_brightness is not a calibration scale. The
current kernel has no applied DC-mode node, so normal PWM is selected. If
dc_mode is exposed in the future it must represent applied panel state.

Capture and timing

AIDL V7 does not return adjusted crop geometry. Capture therefore uses a
reusable full-resolution RGBA buffer with post-processing enabled, and reads
only the sensor region on the CPU. This avoids assumptions about crop support,
alignment or fallback. It costs more writeback bandwidth than a validated ROI
capture and should be measured on hardware before optimizing the allocation.

There is one outstanding request. Each has its own callback and retains its
buffer. A timeout does not permit buffer reuse. After compositor death or an
ambiguous transaction failure the allocation is retired. A live compositor
that never completes an accepted request cannot be cancelled through V7;
the client waits rather than queueing more requests against that allocation.

Capture is limited to 5 Hz, with a refresh request to handle static content.
Sensor sampling uses the stock 50 ms period. Wake samples are suppressed for
300 ms. Sensor events are matched at/after capture completion, with brightness
state at least 20 ms older than the sensor event. Sysfs observation timestamps
approximate panel event time; the current kernel does not expose timestamped
brightness events. Frames from before a power transition are discarded.

Unlike stock's old-frame fallback, capture data older than one second is not
used for a new report. No invented zero-bleed value or uncompensated factory
lux is reported during capture failures. Secure/virtual-display contention,
power transitions, pixel color order, sensor payloads and readback power cost
still require end-to-end device validation.

Validation

From the Android root, with the optional Python unicorn package available:

  python3 device/xiaomi/sm8450-common/sensors/udals/tests/validate.py \
      --dumps /path/to/sm8450

The dump directory contains marble/ and mayfly/. The test compiles the pure
C++ evaluator on the host, executes the original ARM64 math in Unicorn and
compares channel predictions and lux. Android allocation, vector getters,
calibration interpolation, filtering and reporting are stubbed in the oracle;
the tests separately supply calibration ratios/scales and check interpolation,
pixel stride/offset/color order, timeline selection and malformed inputs.
This is not a substitute for a device test of the capture/SSC integration.

Build using breakfast marble (or mayfly), then m sensor-notifier selinux_policy.
Only marble and mayfly select this extension; Zeus retains LightNotifier.
